  "notes": "The ballad-and-midtempo centre of 90s/2000s radio — the biggest catalogue of country power ballads in the era, cut with a rock rhythm section under a conversational baritone. The defining drum move is quoted as dynamic staging: \"cross-stick or hats-only verse, snare and full kit at the chorus, floor-tom or half-time feel for the bridge, then a maximal final chorus,\" with \"kick on 1 and 3 in ballads, 1 / 'and-of-2' / 3 on the rockers\" — so the kick carries both anchors plus a 2& secondary. Harmony is \"the plainest in the genre\": I–V–vi–IV, I–IV–V and vi–IV–I–V, with ii–V only on the countrypolitan-leaning ballads. The melodic shape is the producer's stated casting rule — \"the stylist, more than someone who can do vocal acrobatics\" — so verses are narrow, step-and-third, low-register speech rhythm opening to a chorus a sixth or octave above, which is why `intervals.leap` stays low while the register spans an octave and a half. Bass drop-out in the verse is called in-grammar by the entry, so `followRootsProb` stays high and anticipation low. ⚠ Confidence is medium: the research rates it high-medium and marks the per-track tempo/harmony readings [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the drum densities, the hat fill density and the countermelody ratio — the entry gives staging in words, not numbers, and 0.18 / 0.44 are this author's reading of them.",
